.TH KATAZUKE 1 "2026-10-31" "katazuke" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
katazuke \- remove junk files from a FAT or exFAT disk, then sort its folders
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B katazuke
.br
.B katazuke
.RI [ options ] " device" | mount_point
.br
.B katazuke check
.RI [ options ] " device" | mount_point
.br
.B katazuke doctor
.br
.B katazuke version
.br
.B katazuke help
.SH DESCRIPTION
Many devices list files in the order they sit on the disk, not by name: flash
carts, game loaders, MP3 players and car stereos.
.B katazuke
removes the files that macOS, Windows, Linux and NAS systems leave behind,
then rewrites each folder in sorted order with
.BR fatsort (1).
.PP
A disk is named by its device, such as
.IR /dev/disk4 ,
or by any volume mounted from it, such as
.IR /Volumes/USB ,
which stands for the whole disk. Every FAT and exFAT volume on it is tidied,
even when a single partition such as
.I /dev/disk8s2
is named, after one confirmation that lists them all. Other filesystems on
the disk are left alone. A volume that fails its check stops the run: the
volumes before it stay tidied and the ones after it are untouched.
.PP
Each volume is checked read-only first, and a volume with filesystem errors is
left untouched. Before anything changes, katazuke prints what it is about to
do and asks for the device name to be typed back.
.PP
Names such as
.I System Volume Information
and
.I .Spotlight-V100
are removed only at the top of a volume. Names such as
.IR ._* ,
.I .DS_Store
and
.I Thumbs.db
are removed in every folder.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B check
List the junk files on every volume, changing nothing. A volume that is not
mounted is mounted read-only for the listing, then unmounted again.
.TP
.B doctor
List the tools katazuke needs and whether each is installed. Exits non-zero
only when a required tool is missing.
.TP
.B version
Print the version and the platform.
.TP
.B help
Print the usage page.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-\-natural ", " \-\-no\-natural
Sort numbers by value, so Disc 2 comes before Disc 10. Default yes. Natural
order compares names byte by byte, so a name starting with an accented or
non-Latin letter sorts before every A to Z name on macOS and after them on
Linux arm64. With
.BR \-\-no\-natural ,
they come after every A to Z name on both.
.TP
.BI \-\-order " dirs" | files | mixed
Put folders first, files first, or both together. Default dirs.
.TP
.BR \-\-eject ", " \-\-no\-eject
Eject the disk when done. Default yes.
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-internal
Allow an internal disk. The disk holding the running system is always refused.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print the result as one JSON object on stdout, for scripts. A run reports the
disk, whether it succeeded, how many junk files it removed, whether the disk
was ejected, and each volume. A check reports the disk, whether it is clean,
and the junk files on each volume.
.PP
Every option that takes a value also takes the form
.IR \-\-name = value ,
and
.B \-\-
ends the options.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B KATAZUKE_NATURAL
Set to
.B y
or
.B n
for the same effect as
.B \-\-natural
or
.BR \-\-no\-natural .
.TP
.B KATAZUKE_ORDER
Same as
.BR \-\-order .
.TP
.B KATAZUKE_EJECT
Set to
.B y
or
.B n
for the same effect as
.B \-\-eject
or
.BR \-\-no\-eject .
.TP
.B KATAZUKE_ALLOW_INTERNAL
Set to
.B y
for the same effect as
.BR \-\-allow\-internal .
.TP
.B KATAZUKE_JSON
Set to
.B y
for the same effect as
.BR \-\-json .
.PP
An option on the command line wins over the environment.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when every volume was tidied, or when a check finished, and 1 on any failure
or refusal.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fatsort (1),
.BR kakikomi (1),
.BR kiyome (1)
